it's 30 years this month since one of  the biggest disasters in british  business history  gerald ratner created the world's  largest chain of jewellery stalls  and in one speech he destroyed it  comparing his products to rubbish  he said a pair of earrings were cheaper  than a prawn sandwich and wouldn't last  as long  the speech bombed and his company nearly  collapsed though it wasn't the end of  his business career  we can speak to mr ratner now good  afternoon  to gerald i wonder if we can go back 30  years ago pre  this infamous now albert hall speech  what life what the world of gerald  ratner was like at that time  well we just announced profits of 125  million  the day before in fact we were going for  200 million in the in that year  we had two and a half thousand shops uh  we'd come  from sort of seven years ago a business  that was losing money  so everything was going extremely well  and then i was invited to make the  speech at the albert hall  made a couple of jokes and here we are  talking about it 30 years later  yeah i mean you talk about that the  business had been struggling a few years  previously it was seen as being  inaccessible it's been stuffy you  shifted the focus to affordability  you snapped up rivals you were seen as  having the midas  touch where were you planning to go  pre that speech  well we just uh you know we had a  thousand shops in america  that were doing very well we were one of  the few british retailers to succeed in  america  um we would define the recession as well  because in 1991  uh we were deep in the recession but we  were still doing extremely well  because of the fact that we were  offering um very low prices very  affordable jewelry  um which had never been never happened  before in the jewellery business jewelry  was always regarded as very expensive  the province of the rich we changed all  that we broke the threshold back  that's the sadness of the stupidity  of the speech because all that ended not  only for me but a lot of managers used  to come up to me  you know the age of 21 and say you know  i gave them a chance  um when nobody else would to run a shop  so you know it was a  absolutely unmitigating disaster  consequences of that speech  you must have gone over and over and  over this is countless  times but as you made the remark were  you aware  of how controversial it was going to be  that it was going down badly  not at all in fact it went down very  well everybody at the albert hall  took it the way it was supposed to be  meant that it was just a couple of jokes  to liven up the the speech um  the press said that i said it all about  my jewelry which i never mentioned my  jewelry i said  about one item of sherry decanter that  we acquired when we  bought h samuel um so no it was only  when you read the  tabloids the next day that it did look  terrible but i mean as i left the albert  hall  i didn't think anything was untoward and  um  i don't blame the press because the  press you know  always do that they're disingenuous and  you know good luck to them that's what  people want to read about  that you know they want a story that's  exaggerated somewhat  but um you know it was my own fault it  was a dumb joke to make i've made it  before  and people found it quite funny but on  this occasion  um they didn't find it well they found  it funny in the hall at the albert hall  but uh once they read it in the press or  the press's version of it  they didn't find it funny at all and  people uh stopped coming into our shops  and not only ratners but other  businesses that owned like h samuel  ernest jones watchers of switzerland  and so it had a devastating effect but  yeah and you say it was just meant as a  light-hearted joke but it was  perhaps the timing of this that hit hard  wasn't  it we were in recession people were  struggling to pay bills  you were seen as holding your customers  in contempt and that it was crass and  the result was  you lost everything yeah i did i lost  absolutely everything the shares went  down from four pounds twenty to two  payouts so my house take children out of  school  uh yeah i did i paid a very very high  price for it  but you know if anybody anyone who knows  me and all the managers who i still keep  in touch with and stuff like that  know that the one person that doesn't  have contempt for their customers  is me you know i adored my customers and  i gave them fantastic value for money  and that's what really hurts the fact  that people still list to this day on  twitter  say that um it was a joke  it was a joke that backfired big time  but  this talk about contempt for customers  making fun of them  uh is way off the mark and you're  absolutely right  we were deep in recession in 1991 people  couldn't pay their  electricity bills and stuff like that  and they were looking for a full guy  for this because the mood had changed  dramatically as i said because when i  made the joke  you know two or three years earlier it  was even actually printed in the sun  that same joke and the sons called it  what gerald's gems he's well known for  making jokes they are extremely funny  but that was when we were in a boom when  we were in a recession  they viewed it in a different way what  impact did it  have on you to go from the dizzy heights  to  nothing selling your house losing  everything how did you cope  with that sudden shift  well i didn't cope very well you know  the press said that i was unemployable  everyone was making jokes about me  and i sort of spent seven years lying in  bed watching countdown  i didn't want to play anymore i gave up  it was only when my wife threatened to  kick me out  uh that i went out and i the one thing  that was keeping me sane was cycling  which i still do to this day  25 miles a day um and the exercise has  helped me  through i helped a lot people through  this pandemic  so i really a believer in exercise and  then i started up a health club with no  money whatsoever  sold it two and a half years later four  million pounds  and it was the most wonderful feeling uh  to be back in business and to actually  be able to pay my bills again and not  have huge  bill you know bills that i couldn't pay  and being in this terrible situation  so you know i won't say that i um  i'm glad i made the the joke i far from  it but  i do appreciate things much more  nowadays after losing everything i think  that's human nature  and just quickly you also mentor others  what's your one bit of advice  to them gerald well yes i do do a lot of  mentoring  and i really enjoy it and i like to help  them and i think what i would  say to them is that you know if i can  come back  from the worst corporate disaster of all  time then if you have a setback don't  worry about it you'll come back as well  you know just keep trying you will get  knocked out  nobody will sell through life without  not having a setback  but in fact you know if you do have a  setback to a certain degree  you see people who haven't had setbacks  and they lack a certain amount of  sympathy and empathy  you know to be part of the human race  you've had to have suffered somewhat so  you know don't worry about it if things  go wrong business you can bounce back in  life you can bounce back
 

